NickelOS: virtio-gpu's private ioctls only on a virtio-gpu device.
Sommelier asks the DRM device it was given virtio-gpu questions and counts
on other drivers to fail them: at startup (GETPARAM, RESOURCE_INFO), for
every dma-buf a client attaches (RESOURCE_INFO_CROS, for Crostini's stride
fixups) and as a fallback wait (WAIT). Driver-private ioctl numbers are
per driver: on nvidia-drm, RESOURCE_INFO_CROS (0x45) is its prime fence
context creation, which succeeded and was never undone, so every X11
buffer leaked a fence context in sommelier's device file. After hours (or
a burst: Steam's window after signing in), the GPU ran out of semaphore
surfaces: "Failed to import fence semaphore surface" (ENOMEM), then
Chrome's page flips timed out ("Flip event timeout on head 0"), the screen
froze and Chrome's GPU process was killed by its watchdog, again and
again. Now the device's driver name is checked once (drmGetVersion:
virtio_gpu) and the virtio-gpu paths are skipped elsewhere.

diff --git a/vm_tools/sommelier/compositor/sommelier-dmabuf-sync.cc b/vm_tools/sommelier/compositor/sommelier-dmabuf-sync.cc
index 8a7907b..7e5c247 100644
--- a/vm_tools/sommelier/compositor/sommelier-dmabuf-sync.cc
+++ b/vm_tools/sommelier/compositor/sommelier-dmabuf-sync.cc
@@ -129,6 +129,12 @@ void sl_dmabuf_sync(struct sl_context* ctx, struct sl_sync_point* sync_point) {
     return;
   }
 
+  // NickelOS: the fallback below is virtio-gpu's (see
+  // sl_context.drm_is_virtgpu).
+  if (!ctx->drm_is_virtgpu) {
+    return;
+  }
+
   // Fallback to waiting on a virtgpu buffer's implicit fence.
   //
   // First imports the prime fd to a gem handle. This will fail if this
